It is a very good practice to use POST with a JSON body for filtering, even if it's just a date. It keeps your URLs clean and allows you to easily expand the criteria later without breaking the API contract.
Here is the complete implementation converting both APIs (Date-only and Composite) to POST methods using a JSON body.
1. The Search DTO (DifferenceSearchCriteria.java)
We will use one DTO for both endpoints. For the "Date Only" API, we just ignore the other fields.
package com.fincore.ReportService.dto;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;

@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class DifferenceSearchCriteria {
    private LocalDate reconRunDate;
    private String branchCode;
    private String currency;
    private String cgl;
}

2. The Controller (DifferenceController.java)
I have updated the controller to have two POST mappings. I kept page and size as URL parameters because they are metadata about the response view, not the search criteria itself. This is standard Spring practice.
package com.fincore.ReportService.controller;

import com.fincore.ReportService.dto.DifferenceDTO;
import com.fincore.ReportService.dto.DifferenceSearchCriteria;
import com.fincore.ReportService.service.DifferenceService;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/differences")
public class DifferenceController {

    @Autowired
    private DifferenceService service;

    // 1. Original Get All (unchanged)
    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Page<DifferenceDTO>> getDifferences(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size
    ) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(service.getAllDifferences(page, size));
    }

    // 2. Filter by Date Only (POST)
    @PostMapping("/filter/date")
    public ResponseEntity<Page<DifferenceDTO>> getDifferencesByDate(
            @RequestBody DifferenceSearchCriteria criteria,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size
    ) {
        // We only extract the date from the JSON body
        return ResponseEntity.ok(service.getDifferencesByDate(criteria.getReconRunDate(), page, size));
    }

    // 3. Detailed Filter (POST)
    @PostMapping("/filter/detailed")
    public ResponseEntity<Page<DifferenceDTO>> getDifferencesDetailed(
            @RequestBody DifferenceSearchCriteria criteria,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size
    ) {
        // We extract all fields from the JSON body
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
            service.getDifferencesByComposite(
                criteria.getReconRunDate(),
                criteria.getBranchCode(),
                criteria.getCurrency(),
                criteria.getCgl(),
                page,
                size
            )
        );
    }
}

3. Verification (How to use these APIs)
API 1: Filter by Date
 * Method: POST
 * URL: http://localhost:8080/api/differences/filter/date?page=0&size=50
 * Body (JSON):
   {
    "reconRunDate": "2023-10-27"
}

   (Note: You can include the other fields in the JSON, but the code will ignore them for this specific endpoint.)
API 2: Detailed Filter
 * Method: POST
 * URL: http://localhost:8080/api/differences/filter/detailed?page=0&size=50
 * Body (JSON):
   {
    "reconRunDate": "2023-10-27",
    "branchCode": "BR001",
    "currency": "USD",
    "cgl": "100200"
}

4. Service and Repository (No Changes Needed)
Your Service and Repository layers do not need to change because they simply accept the raw Java types (LocalDate, String, etc.). The Controller is doing the work of "unwrapping" the JSON for them.
 * Service: getDifferencesByDate(LocalDate date, ...)
 * Service: getDifferencesByComposite(LocalDate date, String branch, ...)
This keeps your Service clean and testable without coupling it to the JSON structure.
